00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:01,060
-I just want to read
 you one of my poems.

264
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:01,890
It's "Dear Eliza."

265
00:10:01,890 --> 00:10:03,260
It's--

266
00:10:03,260 --> 00:10:07,020
-I know you don't have a
 license, but look at this.

267
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:08,050
-Now this one's really amusing.

268
00:10:08,050 --> 00:10:09,800
Garfield pulls at Odie's tongue.

269
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:12,140
I love this.

270
00:10:12,140 --> 00:10:15,260
-Yeah, I thought I knew
 something about trucks.

271
00:10:15,260 --> 00:10:17,310
-But I was living
 a horrible lie.

272
00:10:17,310 --> 00:10:18,800
-That seem to fit his job.

273
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:20,890
I didn't know which
 one to concentrate on.

274
00:10:20,890 --> 00:10:21,390
-That's nice.

275
00:10:21,390 --> 00:10:22,680
May I help you, sir?

276
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:24,040
-No.

277
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:26,160
But I'd hate to think that
 sale I made to Old Man Simmons

278
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:29,040
day before yesterday is going
 to have to last me all month.

279
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:29,750
-That's nice.

280
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:32,490
Maybe you'd just like
 to browse the place.

281
00:10:32,490 --> 00:10:34,550
-I just don't seem to get
 the hang of this business.

282
00:10:34,550 --> 00:10:36,300
-The wipers work.

283
00:10:36,300 --> 00:10:36,950
-Oh.

284
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:37,780
No, no.

285
00:10:37,780 --> 00:10:38,960
I don't want to buy a car.

286
00:10:38,960 --> 00:10:39,960
-Wait a minute, wait a minute.

287
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:42,110
Let me-- can I tell you
 about the rear wheel drive?

288
00:10:42,110 --> 00:10:43,350
-How many ways can I say no?

289
00:10:43,350 --> 00:10:47,300
-Wow, this one's about a priest,
 a minister, and a rabbi, see.

290
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:50,090
-No, you're telling it wrong.

291
00:10:50,090 --> 00:10:52,650
-You see, Mr. Brooks, the
 Chevrolet knee-action assembly

292
00:10:52,650 --> 00:10:54,410
is different from
 the Plymouth unit.

293
00:10:54,410 --> 00:10:57,250
-I understand the Plymouth has
 the most expensive construction

294
00:10:57,250 --> 00:10:58,230
of any car.

295
00:10:58,230 --> 00:10:59,150
-Oh, you got me.

296
00:10:59,150 --> 00:11:02,300
-What's the difference
 in the two knee actions?

297
00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:04,280
-I don't know the
 difference, but I

298
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:07,870
do know that
 Chevrolets is better.

299
00:11:07,870 --> 00:11:10,260
-That was the most humiliating
 afternoon I ever had.

300
00:11:10,260 --> 00:11:12,910
I can't believe I
 wet on his couch.

301
00:11:12,910 --> 00:11:16,690
-Maybe I should not have
 dressed as the Angel of Death.

302
00:11:16,690 --> 00:11:19,710
-Hey, nobody buy a
 car, pass it down.

303
00:11:19,710 --> 00:11:20,740
-(Whispering) Nobody buy a car.

304
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:21,810
-(Whispering) Nobody buys a car.

305
00:11:21,810 --> 00:11:23,200
-(Whispering) No cars.

306
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:29,100
-(Singing) I have often walked
 down this street before.

307
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:31,090
-Submitted for you
 approval-- a salesman

308
00:11:31,090 --> 00:11:32,670
who has no [INAUDIBLE].

309
00:11:32,670 --> 00:11:34,540
-I could off myself.

310
00:11:34,540 --> 00:11:36,370
-There's a call on Beacon Street

311
00:11:36,370 --> 00:11:38,780
-Eh, they probably
 failed there too.

312
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:40,250
-It's way over on the
 other side of town.

313
00:11:40,250 --> 00:11:41,000
-I don't have a car

314
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:41,970
-Hey!

315
00:11:41,970 --> 00:11:44,050
-Well, might just as well
 go after it right now.

316
00:11:44,050 --> 00:11:46,160
-Oh, look, there like
 signs on every door now.

317
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:46,960
We have a car.

318
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:48,630
We have a car.

319
00:11:48,630 --> 00:11:50,960
-Hey, Edna Ferber
 and Joseph Kennedy.

320
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:52,240
-And then I became
 an Eagle Scout.

321
00:11:52,240 --> 00:11:55,500
But enough about me, OK, um--

322
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:57,510
-Could you turn down the music?

323
00:11:57,510 --> 00:11:58,670
I can't hear a
 thing you're saying.

324
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:03,740
-But, Mr. Herrington,
 $390 is just

325
00:12:03,740 --> 00:12:05,840
the absolute best we
 can do on your car.

326
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:08,130
-And that's exactly the
 reason why I won't buy.

327
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:10,010
You're too far out of line.

328
00:12:10,010 --> 00:12:13,480
-I knew you'd try to
 make me pay money.

329
00:12:13,480 --> 00:12:17,010
-But Jimmy, we've got five '39
 4- or 2-doors on the lot now.

330
00:12:17,010 --> 00:12:17,980
-Don't you think I know that?

331
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:20,460
I live with that
 every day of my life.

332
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:22,810
-We don't need '39 2-doors.

333
00:12:22,810 --> 00:12:25,650
-OK, I see your side of it.

334
00:12:25,650 --> 00:12:28,540
-I suppose if I sat on my butt
 all day, I'd things that way

335
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:31,170
too.

336
00:12:31,170 --> 00:12:31,670
-Hmm.

337
00:12:31,670 --> 00:12:34,090
I didn't know he
 had a prison record.

338
00:12:34,090 --> 00:12:37,110
Says here he's wanted in Idaho.

339
00:12:37,110 --> 00:12:38,750
-What in the world?

340
00:12:38,750 --> 00:12:40,730
Assault with a deadly weapon?

341
00:12:40,730 --> 00:12:46,040
-AKA the pants-less salesman
 with a piddling peddler?

342
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:47,200
-What?

343
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:49,470
He got a D in trigonometry?

344
00:12:49,470 --> 00:12:49,990
My--

345
00:12:49,990 --> 00:12:51,440
-Oh.

346
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:54,980
-Well, but he is a Pisces
 with the moon in Aries.

347
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:58,210
-Eh, I'd fire him if
 he weren't my son.

348
00:12:58,210 --> 00:13:02,280
[SQUEAKING]

349
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:03,780
-Are you there, Lord?

350
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:05,520
-Hello, wall.

351
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:07,510
-Well, there is a blue
 steak and a pitcher

352
00:13:07,510 --> 00:13:08,970
of Martinis with my name on it.

353
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:14,290
-That's nearly impossible.

354
00:13:14,290 --> 00:13:16,940
No human under my employ
 has ever sold a car.

355
00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:19,790
-Well, I guess no one
 wins the Mathis album.

356
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:29,240
-If I could read, I'd
 know what the problem is.

357
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:30,700
Eh.

358
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:31,430
-There's the problem.

359
00:13:31,430 --> 00:13:33,860
I forgot to carry the two.

360
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:34,890
-Willard Scott?

361
00:13:39,900 --> 00:13:42,460
-Dad, you're smoking
 my kid's bubble pipe.

362
00:13:45,350 --> 00:13:46,850
-(Holding breath)
 Nice night for it.

363
00:13:46,850 --> 00:13:48,880
(Exhales.)

364
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:51,440
-I think it's time for you
 to move out on your own, son.

365
00:13:54,190 --> 00:14:01,010
-Well, at least he's got a nice
 wife to go home-- oh, awkward.

366
00:14:01,010 --> 00:14:02,240
-"Moon for the Misbegotten."

367
00:14:02,240 --> 00:14:06,590
-"Death of a Sales Manager."

368
00:14:06,590 --> 00:14:07,570
-Hmm hmm hmm.

369
00:14:07,570 --> 00:14:08,070
Sandy Frank.

370
00:14:08,070 --> 00:14:12,130
Hey, you're looking
 beefier, son.

371
00:14:12,130 --> 00:14:14,470
-I thought you two boys
 might like some refreshments,

372
00:14:14,470 --> 00:14:16,810
so here's a pitcher
 of nice cold--

373
00:14:16,810 --> 00:14:17,490
-Stoli?

374
00:14:17,490 --> 00:14:19,260
-Lemonade.

375
00:14:19,260 --> 00:14:21,630
-No.

376
00:14:21,630 --> 00:14:22,160
-Thanks, Mother.

377
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:25,460
-And tonight we'll have
 some nice, hot cuddling.

378
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:27,010
-Ahh!

379
00:14:27,010 --> 00:14:29,810
Gravity at work.

380
00:14:29,810 --> 00:14:31,170
-Who's the doughy man
 on our porch steps.

381
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:37,140
-Well, I'll go
 deep fry something.

382
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:43,830
-I'll be upstairs
 in my burlap teddy.

383
00:14:43,830 --> 00:14:47,070
-Got something might important
 on your mind these days.

384
00:14:47,070 --> 00:14:48,000
Haven't you, son?

385
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,180
-Am I that transparent, Dad?

386
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:50,350
-No, not especially.

387
00:14:50,350 --> 00:14:51,380
-So, lay off.

388
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:52,740
-Just the usual things.

389
00:14:52,740 --> 00:14:54,230
-Crushing puppies.

390
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:56,990
-Business, you know.

391
00:14:56,990 --> 00:14:58,310
Sales problems.

392
00:14:58,310 --> 00:14:59,490
Salesmen.

393
00:14:59,490 --> 00:15:02,000
-Now, don't try to
 fool your old dad.

394
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,320
There's something extra
 special worrying you,

395
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:06,180
or I missed my guess.

396
00:15:06,180 --> 00:15:09,670
-You've killed again,
 haven't you, son?

397
00:15:09,670 --> 00:15:10,440
-You know, I was just--

398
00:15:10,440 --> 00:15:11,550
ALL: Ahh!

399
00:15:11,550 --> 00:15:14,340
-Take the average
 young fellow, today.

400
00:15:14,340 --> 00:15:15,490
He won't get out
 and work the way

401
00:15:15,490 --> 00:15:16,710
we used to when I
 started to sell.

402
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:17,830
-Work it, work it, mm-hmm.

403
00:15:17,830 --> 00:15:20,250
-What makes you think that?

404
00:15:20,250 --> 00:15:22,310
-Well, take this young fellow,
 Jimmy Hill, for example.

405
00:15:22,310 --> 00:15:23,540
-OK!

406
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:26,150
-Last month, when I was looking
 for another new car salesman,

407
00:15:26,150 --> 00:15:28,110
he looked to me to
 have twice as much

408
00:15:28,110 --> 00:15:30,410
on the bowl as anybody
 else I could find.

409
00:15:30,410 --> 00:15:32,590
-He looked like a pretty
 good boy to me, too.

410
00:15:32,590 --> 00:15:33,090
-That's right.

411
00:15:33,090 --> 00:15:34,160
Testify, son.

412
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:37,090
-Oh, yes he makes a
 sale now and then.

413
00:15:37,090 --> 00:15:39,630
But that isn't the
 kind of a man I need.

414
00:15:39,630 --> 00:15:40,310
Or want.

415
00:15:40,310 --> 00:15:44,150
-Hasn't the man learned the
 knack of selling, I suppose.

416
00:15:44,150 --> 00:15:44,990
-Huh?

417
00:15:44,990 --> 00:15:45,860
-Well, I guess not.

418
00:15:45,860 --> 00:15:47,520
-Hey, keep it down over there.

419
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:50,000
-Don't you know what
 he's doing wrong?

420
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,120
I can see enough from
 his sales reports.

421
00:15:52,120 --> 00:15:54,920
-Mmm, pretty much
 on his own, then.

422
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:56,510
-Oh, you blame me, huh?

423
00:15:56,510 --> 00:15:59,280
-But he's had a good,
 thorough basic training.

424
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:01,440
I haven't got the time
 to play nursemaid to any

425
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:02,850
of the salesmen.

426
00:16:02,850 --> 00:16:04,250
-May be not, son.

427
00:16:04,250 --> 00:16:07,270
But I wouldn't know about that.

428
00:16:07,270 --> 00:16:09,140
-That's how you
 handle a salesman.

429
00:16:09,140 --> 00:16:11,350
-That isn't what Harry
 Carpenter told me

430
00:16:11,350 --> 00:16:14,170
when you started out to
 work for him 11 years ago.

431
00:16:14,170 --> 00:16:14,670
-J'accuse!

432
00:16:14,670 --> 00:16:16,710
-But I got out and worked.

433
00:16:16,710 --> 00:16:17,510
Hard.

434
00:16:17,510 --> 00:16:20,290
And that's what I'm
 concerned about right now.

435
00:16:20,290 --> 00:16:23,190
Young fellows today won't work
 the way we did 11 years ago.

436
00:16:23,190 --> 00:16:23,820
-Hard.

437
00:16:23,820 --> 00:16:25,930
-Maybe not, son.

438
00:16:25,930 --> 00:16:31,070
And then again, maybe they're
 working harder than you think.

439
00:16:31,070 --> 00:16:34,210
-Oh, food for thought.

440
00:16:34,210 --> 00:16:35,130
-I'll ruminate on that one.

441
00:16:37,870 --> 00:16:43,560
[THEME MUSIC]

442
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:49,910
[THUNDER]

443
00:16:49,910 --> 00:16:50,770
-Ha!

444
00:16:50,770 --> 00:16:54,090
Bella Abzug is actually a
 lot scarier than Bela Lugosi.

445
00:16:54,090 --> 00:16:54,790
-Bigger, too.

446
00:16:54,790 --> 00:16:55,500
-Tor Johnson?

447
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:58,110
Time for going to bed!

448
00:16:58,110 --> 00:17:01,020
-Bram Stoker's "Bride
 of the Monster."

449
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:04,310
-I wonder where
 she's registered.

450
00:17:04,310 --> 00:17:06,020
-Big Tony McCoy.

451
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:07,080
-Harvey B. Dunn?

452
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:09,530
That's the sequel
 to "Johnny Be Good."

453
00:17:09,530 --> 00:17:12,230
-Harvey B. Dunn.

454
00:17:12,230 --> 00:17:13,480
-Hey, which of
 Shatner's "Becwar"

455
00:17:13,480 --> 00:17:14,980
novels did you like best?

456
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:16,170
-I like the second one.

457
00:17:16,170 --> 00:17:17,750
-That's "TekWars."

458
00:17:17,750 --> 00:17:19,360
-Ben Frommer, guide
 to Transylvania?

459
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:24,160
-I wonder who did his
 gowns for this one.

460
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:29,970
-Hey, did you
 notice no Hatfields?

461
00:17:29,970 --> 00:17:30,470
Huh?

462
00:17:30,470 --> 00:17:33,030
Huh?

463
00:17:33,030 --> 00:17:34,080
-I'm dead, Jim.

464
00:17:41,240 --> 00:17:43,600
-Movie Beater Paints.

465
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:45,890
For bad American
 movies, like yours.

466
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:50,390
-Ha.

467
00:17:50,390 --> 00:17:51,070
Pat Dinga.

468
00:17:51,070 --> 00:17:52,000
-He was a Dingaling brother.

469
00:17:54,630 --> 00:17:56,680
-Oh, they're fictitious.

470
00:17:56,680 --> 00:17:58,320
That's no fair.

471
00:17:58,320 --> 00:18:01,300
-I don't know.

472
00:18:01,300 --> 00:18:06,600
-Mr. Wood's wardrobe
 by Mrs. Wood.

473
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:08,310
-Oh my god, Mr. Trevino!

474
00:18:08,310 --> 00:18:11,670
-This is the worst storm yet!

475
00:18:11,670 --> 00:18:13,350
-Hey, it's Bert and Ernie.

476
00:18:17,730 --> 00:18:19,740
-Let's head back
 to the clubhouse.

477
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:21,720
-I'm getting away from
 this lightning catcher

478
00:18:21,720 --> 00:18:22,710
while I still can!

479
00:18:22,710 --> 00:18:25,190
-You'll be soaked to the bone
 before you get ten yards.

480
00:18:25,190 --> 00:18:27,900
-Well, I'd rather be
 soaked than barbecued.

481
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:29,860
-I'd like to be lightly braised.

482
00:18:29,860 --> 00:18:32,470
-Just kiss instead of
 talking to him to death.

483
00:18:35,700 --> 00:18:40,330
-So, what are we
 doing out here again?

484
00:18:40,330 --> 00:18:40,830
-Back here.

485
00:18:40,830 --> 00:18:41,330
Ha.

486
00:18:41,330 --> 00:18:43,800
How did that happen?

487
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:46,770
-[INAUDIBLE]

488
00:18:46,770 --> 00:18:49,240
-It's impossible to make
 it to the main road.

489
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:51,660
Maybe we can make it to
 the old Willows place.

490
00:18:51,660 --> 00:18:53,030
-Are you out of your mind?

491
00:18:53,030 --> 00:18:56,380
-No, but thanks for asking.

492
00:18:56,380 --> 00:18:58,370
-The newspaper stories say--

493
00:18:58,370 --> 00:18:59,850
-Oh, act your age man.

494
00:19:03,330 --> 00:19:06,010
-What the-- son of a gun,
 we're back here again.

495
00:19:06,010 --> 00:19:08,430
How'd we do that?

496
00:19:08,430 --> 00:19:10,420
-Don't look good to me.

497
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:11,870
-Ah, you're just spooked.

498
00:19:11,870 --> 00:19:12,850
-Spooked enough.

499
00:19:12,850 --> 00:19:14,790
That place just
 don't look healthy.

500
00:19:14,790 --> 00:19:17,210
-It hasn't been
 lived in in 15 years.

501
00:19:17,210 --> 00:19:17,710
It's deserted.

502
00:19:17,710 --> 00:19:19,180
-It is, eh?

503
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:21,110
-I still want to have
 a picnic, dammit.

504
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:28,320
-Hey, it's Boo Radley's house.

505
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:29,760
-You said this
 place was deserted.

506
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:30,660
-Well, it's supposed to be.

507
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:32,640
I don't get it.

508
00:19:32,640 --> 00:19:34,160
-Hi, it's Curt Gowdy.

509
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:36,070
Can me and Phil Harris come in?

510
00:19:36,070 --> 00:19:40,270
-No, I don't want a Chevrolet.

511
00:19:40,270 --> 00:19:41,350
-What do you want?

512
00:19:41,350 --> 00:19:44,300
-Oh, your old hip shipment's
 in, Mr. Legosi, sir.

513
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:45,940
-I didn't think
 anybody lived here.

514
00:19:45,940 --> 00:19:47,040
-So, here's an idea.

515
00:19:47,040 --> 00:19:49,440
-We thought maybe we
 could get out of the rain.

516
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:51,530
Looks like it's going
 to last all night.

517
00:19:51,530 --> 00:19:53,030
-That's pretty bad out there.

518
00:19:53,030 --> 00:19:53,530
-Oh, no.

519
00:19:53,530 --> 00:19:54,660
You can't stay.

520
00:19:54,660 --> 00:19:55,690
-Thanks for coming by, though.

521
00:19:55,690 --> 00:19:57,260
-You can't stay.

